09:57:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Structure fires usually don’t cause a “fuse” of explosions traveling back through gas pipes because gas inside an intact gas line does not have enough oxygen to burn. For combustion or an explosion, natural gas needs to mix with air in a fairly narrow range—roughly 5–15% gas in air. Inside a service pipe or main, it is mostly gas under pressure, with little or no oxygen. If a fire damages a pipe, gas may escape, mix with air outside the pipe, and burn as a jet flame or create an explosion in an enclosed space. But that flame generally cannot propagate backward into the gas-filled pipe because there is no oxygen there to sustain it. Other reasons include: - **Gas flow is outward.** Pressurized gas is flowing from the utility system toward the leak, which tends to oppose flame traveling upstream. - **Pipes, regulators, and valves break up the system.** Buildings usually have service shutoff valves, meters, pressure regulators, and branch lines. Utilities can also isolate sections of their distribution network. - **Fire usually melts or ruptures small pipes rather than detonating them.** The result is often a localized gas-fed flame, not a series of pipe explosions. - **A main is not an explosive container by default.** It would need the right gas-air mixture and confinement to explode. A normal gas main is intended to contain gas, not a combustible gas-air mixture. That said, gas fires are still serious. A fire can damage a line, create a sustained flame, or allow gas to accumulate in a building and explode. Firefighters commonly shut off gas service when it is safe, and gas utilities may shut down or isolate nearby mains. Rare “flashback” events can occur under unusual conditions, but they do not normally travel all the way to the gas source or cause a citywide chain reaction.
